// stdlib/unorm.sa -- canonical Unicode normalization (NFC/NFD, prefix `un_`).
//
// Dependency: import stdlib/utf8.sa BEFORE this module. Like money.sa over
// decimal.sa, this module names its dependency's functions without importing
// it, because the module loader does not dedupe a diamond and a second import
// would declare every u8_ function twice.
//
// What this is. Canonical normalization (Unicode Standard Annex #15) puts
// text into one of two comparable forms: NFD fully decomposes every character
// into its base plus combining marks and then puts each run of marks in
// canonical order, and NFC recomposes what it can. Two strings that differ
// only in how they were typed -- a precomposed e-acute versus e plus U+0301,
// an Ohm sign versus Greek omega, a Hangul syllable versus its Jamo -- become
// byte-identical in either form, which is what `un_equal` tests.
//
// What this is not. There is no NFKC/NFKD here, so compatibility characters
// are never folded: the ligature fi, superscript two and fullwidth A pass
// through NFC unchanged. There is no case folding, no collation and no bidi
// reordering. Normalization is canonical only, by design.
//
// Failure contract: text functions return "" and predicates return -1 for
// malformed or over-long input, exactly like stdlib/ugraph.sa. The list
// functions return [] when any element is not a scalar value. U+0000 is a
// valid scalar for the list API but cannot be carried in a Sayanox string,
// so the text API can never see it; u8_from refuses it on the way back.
//
// The tables below are generated; see the marker comment. They come from the
// Unicode 17.0.0 Character Database files themselves (UnicodeData.txt for
// canonical decompositions and combining classes, DerivedNormalizationProps.txt
// for Full_Composition_Exclusion), compressed to flat lists and searched by
// bisection. Hangul syllables are arithmetic, not tabulated, like the LV/LVT
// classes in stdlib/ugraph.sa.
//
// Verification: selfhost/stdlib_unorm_test.sa runs all 20034 cases of the
// official Unicode 17.0.0 NormalizationTest.txt through the real NFC/NFD
// code, plus API expectations from an independent Python reference that is
// itself proven against the same 20034 cases first. A compact companion
// (stdlib_unorm_compact_test.sa) carries the same rule set to native_aot.

// Cap shared with stdlib/utf8.sa: a wider input is refused, not truncated.
make un_max_bytes() -> num {
  give 65536
}

// Largest code point count whose properties are tabulated.
make un_max_cp() -> num {
  give 1114111
}

// Is cp inside any [lo, hi] pair of a flat range list? Bisection.
make un_in_ranges(tbl: list, cp: num) -> num {
  hold n = len(tbl)
  hold lo = 0
  hold hi = u8_div(n, 2) - 1
  while lo <= hi {
    hold mid = u8_div(lo + hi, 2)
    hold a = tbl[mid * 2]
    hold b = tbl[mid * 2 + 1]
    when cp < a {
      hi = mid - 1
    } otherwise {
      when cp > b {
        lo = mid + 1
      } otherwise {
        give 1
      }
    }
  }
  give 0
}

